If You Find This

An ensemble war film told through cameras recovered in Ukraine, Myanmar, and Israel and Gaza. Some come home in a friend’s pocket. One passes through three strangers before it reaches a family. One is recovered from a body by someone the person wearing it would have considered the enemy.

The people in the film never meet. We get to know them the way you get to know the men in Band of Brothers: you learn who complains about the food, whose jokes everybody pretends to hate, and who quietly does somebody else’s share of the work. Here, we learn all that mostly from waist-high footage of people who assumed nobody would ever watch it.

The first time we see each camera, somebody else is holding it. Then we go back to when it belonged to someone.

Where	Whose footage we see	How it gets home
Ukraine	A soldier whose little girl keeps asking him to film snow. His bunkmate keeps ruining the shots by walking through them in his underwear.	The bunkmate brings the camera to the soldier’s apartment. Only after the daughter asks him to stay and watch does he realize how often his own voice is on it.
Myanmar	A young resistance fighter who lends her camera to her brother to film a family lunch, then spends half the meal telling him how to use it.	Months later, a stranger carries the memory card to the family. Her mother recognizes the camera strap before she recognizes the person delivering it. She stitched it herself.
Israel and Gaza	An Israeli reservist films ordinary life between call-ups. Separately, a Palestinian nursing student records short clips for his sister from a borrowed action camera, sometimes turning it around so she can see him.	The reservist’s camera is taken from his body and handed over by someone who never knew him. The student’s sister gets his camera back from a friend and finds a clip in which he asks her to settle a stupid argument. She’s furious that he’s right.

I’d give each place a sustained chapter and let the audience get comfortable there. The temptation would be to cut between firefights and make them rhyme. I’d cut between moments like the bunkmate ruining the snow shot and the mother sewing the strap. Each conflict keeps its own history and consequences, and the people get enough time on screen to become specific.

The opening

Black screen. A zipper. A chair scrapes across a kitchen floor.

A man in a good shirt with an old stain on the cuff sets a camera on the table. A little girl asks if it still works. He says yes before he checks.

GIRL
Did he get the snow?

Cut to the camera footage. It’s snowing through an open window. Somewhere below, a dog is barking. A bare leg steps into frame.

FATHER, BEHIND THE CAMERA
Get out of the way. It’s for my daughter.

The man steps back out. After a beat, his hand reaches in to move a dirty cup off the windowsill.

BUNKMATE
Start it again. I’ve cleaned up.

In the kitchen, the girl laughs. The man beside her doesn’t. Then, half a second later, he does.

The father had started recording again. She wants to see this one twice.

⸻

I’d make it as fiction with actors and original footage, including all the recovered recordings. We’d spend most of the film inside those cameras and come out only when someone has to decide what to do with one. That gives the living people a story of their own. A friend has to decide whether to stay while a child watches. A sister has to decide whether to send a clip to the rest of the family. A stranger has to decide whether returning a camera means sharing what’s on it.

The last recovered clip would be from the Myanmar fighter’s family lunch. She takes the camera from her brother, sets it down badly, and for the first time we see her whole face. Somebody offscreen asks her to taste the soup. She does, says it needs salt, and immediately three people tell her she’s wrong.

We let the argument run. She’s laughing when the clip ends.